        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The addressee possesses authoritative and reliable intelligence insights due to their committee position.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The United States should act decisively when responding to foreign aggression like Russia’s invasion of Ukraine.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Intelligence assessments are credible indicators of strategic developments in warfare.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There exists a meaningful connection between legislative action and battlefield outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts the addressee’s judgment and integrity despite potential classification constraints.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 43779,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43779.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "We've got to get this passed. We've got to continue to support the Ukrainian people. I think it can make a real difference and make no mistake. It also goes towards modernizing our own defense munitions base, which is critical as well as we face threats across the world. So I think you can see real movement here. And that's important because Ukraine has been so incredibly successful. So you see that they've held key, despite reports that it would be taken over in a matter of days once Russia invaded. You see them holding onto that Eastern manufacturing area where 90% of Soviet era munitions were manufactured. That's been critical and they've restarted some of that munitions-based manufacturing. You see them holding onto the port of Odessa even after the Russians withdrew their support for the the deal that they had made to get grain shipments out. Russia backed away from that deal. But Ukraine continued getting grain shipments out through Odessa. So that's a critical port for them to hold. So all of this shows that the Ukrainians have done an amazing job holding on to their country, holding their country together. And I think with US support through this supplemental, they can make real gains. They have lost ground. And it's been devastating. I have a large Ukrainian-American community. In my district, we have Ukrainian soldiers who come to seek medical treatment in my area, and talking to a gentleman who lost both legs and his eyes, because he was standing in the breach trying to ensure the Russians didn't take over a town behind him, because he's seen the devastation that causes and what they do to women, the kidnapping of children, the raising of villages. He felt like he had to stand there. Without proper munitions, we are going to see continued movement by the Russians in areas that we desperately need the Ukrainians to hold.",
