  {
    "episode_id": 43779,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43779.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "So, you know, when you look at the indictment of Senator Menendez from my State of New Jersey, you see that he gave away secrets to a foreign entity, a foreign power on personnel at the embassy, which is very dangerous and endangers our personnel. And as the chair of the Foreign Affairs Committee in the Senate, he would have been well aware of that. So then to see that he continues to receive intelligence briefings in the United States Senate was really appalling to me. He'd already possibly endangered some people serving our government overseas and then to see that he might continue to have that ability, justified logic. And then we started to hear that for 50 years, this is the time when for 50 years by tradition, the intelligence community starts to brief someone who is a credible presidential candidate. And to think that someone like Trump, who even while president was giving away state secrets to foreign adversaries, and then once he wasn't president was holding on to secret documents and not turning them over when asked, and then trying to hide that offense, that we would simply give him intelligence briefings. Again, defied logic. And this was just by tradition. This was, you know, in no way, something that we had to do by law. So that's why I put this forward. Alyssa Slotkin, another member of the House Armed Services Committee and someone I work with and national security issues is co-sponsoring the legislation. And we're going to, you know, we've sent it out to other offices and I think we'll get a great deal of support for it.",
